## 3. Probe results

1. The effective-choice fallback cannot post a value outside the enum. class-session-control.tsx:59-69: state is set only from the rendered choices (all enum members); effectiveLifetime falls back to '3h', an in-enum value; the post body at :76 sends effectiveLifetime. The server stays the authority: contracts.ts:38-47 refuses any value outside the enum, and class-session.ts:49-68 throws invalid_input for unknown values. New tests at class-session-control.test.tsx:390-420 pin the fallback and the error clear.
2. The class-session.ts change is formatting only. The joined 'let row ... ; try {' became two lines (:169-170). computeSessionExpiry, the refusal placement before the first row write (:125-129), the audit metadata (:180), and the insert values (:171-174) are behavior-equivalent to the round-1 version audited at 9e2bc7492. contracts.ts:37-41 is JSDoc wording only.
3. The message changes hold no markup injection and no secret. The new TeacherManual.startClass.step4 at en.json:4380, th.json:4380, cn.json:4380, tw.json:4380, vi.json:4229 uses <ui>{endClass}</ui>, the pattern of 47 existing manual strings. manual-guide.tsx:90 renders t.rich with a fixed tag map (ui -> UiLabel); message text never reaches HTML parsing. The text holds no code, hash, or credential.
4. F142 from a security view: after a reload the panel resets to '3h' (:59), and New code posts '3h', so the server closes the 7-day session and opens a 3-hour one. This only shortens the credential window. The actor is the authenticated teacher of the class; the action is deliberate and audited (class_end 'replaced' plus class_start with lifetime). It cannot extend a session, leak a secret, or cross a tenant. Verdict: usability point only, not a security risk. The D-5 default (no change now) is acceptable from a security standpoint.

